# Migration Skill Fixes

Findings from running `webflow-sanity:migration` §1–§7 end to end against Lux Radiology,
written down so they can be fed back into the skill rather than rediscovered on the next
site.

Three kinds of finding:

- **Engine bugs** — defects in `migration-scripts/`, now patched in this repo. The skill
  says the engine is copied *as-is* and never edited per site, so each one is a debt to
  push upstream and then re-sync.
- **Scaffold gaps** — no engine change needed, but §2 does not mention them and each cost
  time or shipped a hazard.
- **Process gaps** — the method itself was wrong or silent about something, independent of
  any code.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Every item below is a **general** Webflow/Astro/Sanity problem, not a Lux quirk. That is
> the argument for upstreaming rather than leaving local patches.

## The one that matters most

### P1. `isArchived || isDraft` is the WRONG published test

**Process gap. Highest priority on this list.** It silently deletes live URLs.

The skill states plainly that "`isArchived` and `isDraft` are Webflow's own flags and are
the honest test for 'not live'". On this site that rule is wrong, and wrong in the
direction that loses content:

`service/ultrasound-guided-injections` has `isDraft: true` **and**
`lastPublished: "2026-05-29"`. Webflow carries on serving an item that was published and
later flipped back to draft — the page is live, it renders, and it is in the live
sitemap. The naive rule drops it, taking a real URL out of a migration whose entire
acceptance test is URL parity.

`lastPublished` is the discriminator. The site's three *other* drafts
(`immigration-x-rays`, `3d4d`, `ultrasound`) all have `lastPublished: null` — never
published, correctly dropped.

```js
export const isPublished = (item) =>
  !item.isArchived && (!item.isDraft || item.lastPublished != null);
```

Verified: this reproduces the live sitemap **exactly** for all five public collections
(post 3, service 7, team 3, category 2, review 169). The naive rule gets `service` wrong
by one — and nothing downstream would have noticed, because 6 documents producing 6 pages
is internally consistent.

The skill's §4 should carry this as the default rule, with the naive form named as the
trap.

## Engine bugs

### E1. A shell region with no class of its own

**High.** Silent failure mode.

`SHELL` addressed nav and footer as `{tag, cls}`, and `findSpan` requires the element to
carry the class itself. Lux's entire navigation region is an **unclassed** wrapper:

```html
<body><div><div class="mobile-navigation … w-nav"> …
```

That bare `<div>` holds five siblings — mobile navbar, desktop navbar, sticky scroll
navbar, mobile nav section, parking alert bar. No class covers the set, so ~10KB of nav
duplicates into every page's main fragment and the page still renders perfectly.

**Fixed here** with a second addressing mode, `{tag, childCls}` — the innermost `tag`
wrapping an element with class `childCls`.

| File | Change |
|---|---|
| `lib/html.mjs` | new `findSpanWrapping(spans, tag, childCls)` |
| `extract-webflow-page.mjs` | `findShell()` / `describeShell()`; SHELL lookups route through them |

Two properties the upstream version must keep:

**STRICT ancestry.** Written first with `s.start <= marker.start`, which matches the
marker *itself* (a `div` inside a `div`) and, being innermost, wins. `nav.html` came out
at **2,897 bytes instead of 10,278** — the mobile navbar alone, with the rest left
duplicated in all 18 fragments. It rendered perfectly; the only signal was a shared file
too small to be a nav. Same class of bug as the `\bfooter\b` story already in
`html.mjs`'s header, reached by a different route — worth adding as a second worked
example there.

**INNERMOST, not outermost** — the opposite of `findSpan`. Every ancestor up to `<body>`
contains the marker, so the outermost match is whatever top-level wrapper a page happens
to have. `/book` wraps its whole page in `div.page_wrapper`, so outermost would have
returned the entire page on that one route and the nav region on the other seventeen.

### E2. The non-ASCII literals in `test-converter.mjs` had been flattened

**High.** A check that could never pass, and one that could never fail.

Two patterns in the nbsp assertion were written as literal characters and had been
silently turned into ordinary spaces somewhere in the file's history:

```js
if (/&nbsp;| /.test(sourceAll)) {              // ← second alternative is a plain space
  check('no literal nbsp survives',
        !portableTextToPlain(all).includes(' '),  // ← a plain space
```

So the guard matched any fixture containing **any space** (always), and the assertion
demanded the text contain **no space at all** (never). The check failed on every corpus
for every site, reporting "Webflow spacing was not normalised" while the converter was
handling `&nbsp;` correctly all along.

**Fixed** by using `' '` / `'‌'` escapes. A non-ASCII character that carries
meaning must never be written as a literal byte in engine source.

### E3. Fixture guards match across file boundaries

**Medium.** Demands a construct the corpus does not contain.

`test-converter.mjs` and `test-portable.mjs` both build `sourceAll` by joining every
fixture, then guard with patterns containing `[\s\S]*?`:

```js
if (/<li[^>]*>[\s\S]*?<(ul|ol)\b/i.test(sourceAll)) { … }
```

An `<li>` in one fixture happily matches a `<ul>` in a *different* fixture, so the suite
asserted nested-list behaviour on a corpus with zero nested lists. **Fixed** with a
per-fixture `anySource(re)` helper in both files. Single-tag guards can keep using
`sourceAll`.

### E4. `jsdom` is an undeclared engine dependency

**Medium.** `sanity/html-to-portable-text.mjs` imports it; nothing installs it. `npm test`
dies with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` instead of its intended, informative refusal about
missing fixtures. `jsonc-parser` has the same requirement and *is* documented — `jsdom`
just needs the same line.

### E5. The asset localiser's URL patterns, both of them

**High.** Ten assets reported as Webflow 403s that were all local bugs. All ten fetch 200
once the URL is correct.

**HTML pattern over-captured.** Webflow writes CSS into HTML `style` attributes, where the
`url(...)` quotes are HTML-escaped:

```html
style="background-image:url(&quot;https://…/icon.svg&quot;)"
```

`[^"'\s]+` sees no `"` there — `&`, `q`, `u`, `o`, `t`, `;` and `)` are all allowed — so
it swallowed `&quot;)` onto the end of six URLs.

**CSS pattern truncated.** `[^"')\s]+` stops at the first `)`, including one inside a
filename. `clock%20(1).png` became `…clock%20(1` for four assets. The skill's own comment
warns about parentheses in Webflow filenames and says "the HTML pattern must NOT exclude
`)`" — but the CSS pattern has the same exposure and it is the one that bit.

**Fixed** with a shared `TERM` alternation that includes `&quot;`/`&#34;`/`&apos;`/`&#39;`,
and a CSS pattern that consumes `)` when more filename follows and terminates only on a
`)` followed by end-of-value punctuation.

### E6. Webflow's asset CDN is not in the Webflow-host list

**High.** A site can pass the audit while still loading images from Webflow.

`d3e54v103j8qbb.cloudfront.net` is Webflow's static asset CDN. It was absent from both
`WEBFLOW_HOSTS` in `check-assets.mjs` and `WF_HOST` in the localiser, and it is easy to
miss precisely because the hostname says "cloudfront" and nothing in it says "webflow". On
this site it served a background SVG from inside the ported stylesheet.

**Fixed** in both, matched by literal bucket id — a blanket `*.cloudfront.net` would also
sweep up a client's own distribution.

### E7. `check-assets.mjs` reported assets missing that were present

**Medium.** Two independent causes, same symptom.

- The `url()` branch of `LOCAL_REF` excluded `)`, truncating `…clock-(1).png` to
  `…clock-(1` — then failing the exists-on-disk check against a file sitting right there,
  correctly referenced. Four assets.
- `decodeURIComponent` ran before the disk probe. Webflow is inconsistent about encoding
  its own filenames, and `localName` decodes exactly once: a singly-encoded name lands on
  disk as `clock-(1).png`, a **doubly** encoded one as the literal
  `wilson%20car%20park%20(Medium).jpeg`, percent signs and all. Decoding again invented a
  phantom missing file.

**Fixed:** four alternatives in `LOCAL_REF` (attribute, `url("…")`, `url('…')`, bare
`url(…)`), and the disk probe now accepts the raw **or** decoded form.

### E8. `check-assets.mjs` does not scan JavaScript

**Low, but worth stating.** `files` covers `.html` and `.css` only, so Webflow URLs inside
ported JS are invisible to the "no Webflow-hosted assets" gate. On this site two
"Made in Webflow" badge SVGs remain inside a minified runtime chunk.

Left as a documented residual rather than fixed: the badge is absent from the live DOM and
from ours (Webflow ships the logic to every site and only renders it on free plans), so the
code path is unreachable, and rewriting a minified vendor chunk is a worse risk than the
residual. But the engine should at least **report** JS hits so the decision is conscious.

### E9. `check-sitemap.mjs` canonical check was attribute-order dependent

**High.** It produced a false failure *and* a vacuous pass simultaneously.

```js
const canonicalOf = (html) => html.match(/<link rel="canonical" href="([^"]*)"/)?.[1];
```

Webflow writes the attributes the other way round — `<link href="…" rel="canonical"/>` —
so this matched nothing on a correctly ported site. "every page has a canonical" failed on
all 202 pages, and "every page is its own canonical" **passed vacuously** because it had
no hrefs to compare and therefore could never fail.

A check that cannot fail is worse than no check, because it reads as reassurance. **Fixed**
by finding the tag first, then the `href` within it, both case-insensitively.

### E10. No command for the §6 image check

**Medium.** The checklist has the row and ships nothing to satisfy it.

The skill asks for "list the rendered pages and diff one image per page" by hand — which
is exactly why it gets skipped, and the bug it catches (text bound per item, images not)
is the one it calls most likely to ship.

**Added here** as `migration-scripts/check-cms-images.mjs` (`npm run cms:images`), now part
of `preflight`. Per collection it asserts the page count, that every page references at
least one Sanity image, and that no two pages share an identical image set — with declared
exceptions carrying a reason, because genuine duplicates exist (two Lux posts really do
share one main image).

It earned its place immediately by flagging that pair, which then had to be verified
against the raw dump rather than waved through.

## Scaffold gaps

### S1. Astro 7 + the Cloudflare adapter split the build output

**High.** Breaks three engine scripts and the Wrangler config.

The skill says "Astro 5 `output: 'static'` with the Cloudflare adapter". On Astro 7 the
adapter defaults `build.client` to `./client/`, so pages land in
`web/dist/client/about.html` — while `verify-port.mjs`, `check-assets.mjs` and
`check-sitemap.mjs` all compute `join(WEB, 'dist')` and expect `<page>.html` directly
inside it. Pointing Wrangler at `./web/dist` instead serves an empty directory and 404s
every route **with nothing in the logs**.

**Fixed** with one line rather than forking three engine files:

```js
build: {format: 'file', client: './', server: './_server/'}
```

`_server` then needs an `.assetsignore` entry.

### S2. The adapter prerenders in workerd, which has no filesystem

**High.** This is the one that will ambush the next site in §3, not §2.

`prerenderEnvironment` defaults to `'workerd'`. Prerender then runs in a sandbox whose cwd
is `/bundle` with **no repository filesystem at all**. The first build failed with
`Could not locate project.jsonc … (/bundle)`, which reads like a path bug and is a sandbox
boundary.

> [!CAUTION]
> Far bigger than the `project.jsonc` read. The engine assumes Node semantics throughout —
> `extract` writes to `web/src/html/` and `web/src/data/`, and the verifiers read `dist`
> from disk. Anything touching `node:fs` during prerender fails identically.

**Fixed, belt and braces:** `prerenderEnvironment: 'node'`, config values inlined via Vite
`define` from `astro.config.mjs` (the last point where Node's `fs` exists), and page
fragments loaded with `import.meta.glob(…, {query: '?raw', eager: true})` so Vite bundles
them.

### S3. The adapter writes `wrangler.json` into the assets directory

**Medium.** Publishes configuration if unignored — it is fetchable at `/wrangler.json`,
listing bindings and account-shaped config. §8 covers `.assetsignore` for `_worker.js` and
`_routes.json` only. Current adapter versions *do* append `wrangler.json` and `.dev.vars`
themselves, so the built file legitimately lists it twice if you also add it — worth saying,
both so the duplicate is not read as a mistake and so nobody relies on someone else's
default to prevent a config leak.

### S4. `.gitignore`'s bare `.env` does not cover what the guard reads

**High.** Secrets-shaped files were committable.

Git matches a bare `.env` as an exact filename, but
`guard-production-deploy.mjs` explicitly scans `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production` and
`.env.production.local`. Three of the four were not ignored. Anything the guard reads is a
file that can exist, and therefore one that can be committed.

```
.env
.env.*
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
!.env.example
!.dev.vars.example
```

No leading slashes, so these match at any depth and `studio/.env` is covered by the same
rules. The negations are required or the committed templates vanish too.

### S5. `migration/raw/` is documented as gitignored and is not

**High.** Personal data into git history, permanently.

`pull.mjs`'s own docstring says "migration/raw/ is gitignored: it is large, re-pullable".
Nothing in the scaffold adds the rule. On this site that would have committed 216 review
items — named individuals' review text and their Google avatar URLs — into history, where
deleting it later does not help.

The skill should add `migration/raw/` to the ignore guidance, and should say out loud that
the converted NDJSON *is* intended to be committed so that choice is made knowingly.

### S6. `astro check` typechecks the ported Webflow bundles

**Medium.** `allowJs` has to be on (the config reader is `.mjs`) and Astro's strict
tsconfig uses `include: ["**/*"]`, so `web/public/wf/js/*.js` — Webflow's minified jQuery
and webpack chunks — get type-checked. ~215KB of diagnostics about code we did not write,
and slow enough to look hung. One line: `"exclude": ["dist", "public/wf"]`.

### S7. `.wrangler/` is not mentioned

**Low.** `astro build` (via the adapter) and any `wrangler` invocation, including
`--dry-run`, create `web/.wrangler/` holding miniflare's emulated KV/R2/D1/cache sqlite
files. ~20 untracked files. Belongs beside `.astro/` and `.sanity/`.

### S8. `sanity deploy` has no dry run for a Studio

**Low.** Documentation accuracy. The acceptance list says to run
`npm run studio:deploy -- --dry-run` "if supported". On the current CLI `--dry-run` exists
**only** for App SDK app deployments (`sanity deploy --create --title … --dry-run`), never
for a Studio. So the fallback is always the path taken — verify config, build and help, and
state that hosting permissions and hostname availability remain unverified. Worth stating
definitively so nobody hunts for a flag that is not there, or substitutes a real deploy.

### S9. Two Sanity API deprecations in the scaffold shape

**Low.** Both warn on every build; both are one line.

| Deprecated | Current | Where |
|---|---|---|
| `autoUpdates: false` | `deployment: {autoUpdates: false}` | `studio/sanity.cli.ts` |
| default export of `@sanity/image-url` | named `createImageUrlBuilder` | the site's Sanity client |

### S10. The adapter provisions an unused `IMAGES` binding

**Low.** A ported Webflow site never asks Astro to process an image — the pages carry
Webflow's own `<img>` markup verbatim and CMS images come from Sanity's CDN with its own
transforms. The binding then has to exist for a deploy to validate, for nothing.
`imageService: 'passthrough'` removes it. (A `SESSION` KV binding is still added with no
apparent opt-out; inert with no Worker, so left alone.)

## Process gaps

### P2. Rich text must be bound BEFORE short text values

**High.** Cost a build failure that looked like a template problem.

`bindText` replaces every occurrence of the sample's value — correctly, since Webflow
renders the same field in several places. But the team template's sample is Alan, and his
bio opens *"Alan is an experienced X-ray technician…"*. Binding the name first rewrote the
name **inside the bio**, after which the rich-text region no longer began with the text
used to locate it, and `/team/andrew` failed.

Replace rich-text regions wholesale first; bind short values afterwards. §6 should say so
— it is not obvious, and the failure names the wrong culprit.

### P3. Locate rich-text regions by CONTENT, not by index

**Medium.** A positional index is a coin-flip that renders convincingly either way.

The service template has **six** `.w-richtext` regions: the service description, the
category's booking note, and four pieces of static furniture repeated across several
templates. Which index is which is not obvious, not stable across a re-extraction, and
wrong in a way that still looks like prose.

Matching on the sample's own rendered text makes the binding say what it means, and turns a
template change into a build failure instead of a content swap. Worth promoting from
technique to instruction in §6.

### P4. The same CMS field renders as `<img>` on one template and CSS background on another

**Medium.** `category.image` is a `background-image:url(&quot;…&quot;)` on
`div.hero-image`; `category.icon`, immediately beside it, is an `<img>`. Same content
model, two mechanisms, and only the template says which. The first attempt used an
`<img>` binder for both and failed the build on `/category/x-ray` — the correct outcome,
but §6 should warn that a background-image binder is also needed, and that the escaped
quotes bite there too.

### P5. A field can appear in the template by coincidence

**Medium.** `service.shortName` ("Pregnancy Scans") occurred exactly once in its
template — inside the description's own price list, as a line of the client's pricing copy.
It is not rendered on a service detail page at all; it is used in the nav dropdown and the
cards, which live in the shared shell.

Binding it would have rewritten a line of real pricing copy on six pages. A single
occurrence is not evidence of a bind point — check *where* it occurs.

### P6. A collection's listing can filter on a field, invisibly

**Medium.** `/reviews` renders only reviews rated **4 and above**: zero of the 17
published reviews rated below 4 appear, while all 152 rated 4+ are eligible (capped at 101
by Webflow's list limit). The low-rated reviews keep their own pages and their live URLs.

Nothing in the content model says this. It was found by diffing the rendered listing
against the dump and noticing one review missing from an otherwise perfect date-descending
sequence. §6 should prompt for it: a listing whose item count does not match the
collection count is filtered, and the filter is part of the port.

### P7. Collection ordering may exist nowhere in the API

**Medium.** The skill warns about "a collection with NO order field, whose order lived
only in the parent's reference array". Lux's team collection is worse: `seq` is null on
every published member, there is no parent, and **Webflow's manual collection sort is not
exposed by its API at all**.

The rendered order (`andrew, alan, nethanel`) is neither alphabetical nor creation order,
so it is genuinely a manual sort and genuinely unrecoverable from any dump. It had to be
read off the rendered page and committed as a constant, then asserted in `verify.mjs`.

§4 should name this third case and say that reading the rendered page is the legitimate
last resort.

### P8. Webflow's own JSON-LD can be invalid

**Low, but free to fix.** The review template's JSON-LD interpolates the review body raw,
and bodies contain newlines — so the string literal is unterminated and the block is
invalid JSON on the live site. Regenerating structured data from the document rather than
string-substituting into the frozen block fixes it for every page at no cost, and avoids
the separate risk of patching four per-document values by hand and forgetting one.

### P9. Dates must be bound on the RENDERED string, in Webflow's locale

**Low.** The post template contains "April 16, 2023"; the ISO value appears nowhere.
Formatting with `en-NZ` produces "16 April 2023" — arguably more correct for an Auckland
clinic, and not what the published pages say. Matching the port comes first. Also set
`timeZone: 'UTC'` or a midnight post shifts a day depending on where the build runs.

## Suggested upstream order

1. **P1** — the published test. It silently deletes live URLs and nothing downstream
   notices.
2. **E2**, **E9** — checks that cannot fail are worse than absent checks.
3. **E5**, **E6**, **E7** — §5 is the step the skill calls non-optional, and three
   separate defects made it report local bugs as Webflow's fault.
4. **S1**, **S2** — Astro 7 reality; S2 ambushes §3, not §2.
5. **S4**, **S5** — one leaves secrets committable, the other commits personal data.
6. **E1**, **E3**, **E4**, **E10**, **P2**, **P3** — each cost real time on first
   encounter.
7. Everything else — tidy-ups.

> [!NOTE]
> Once the engine fixes land upstream, re-sync `migration-scripts/` and drop the local
> patches so this repo stops carrying a fork. Current divergence:
> `lib/html.mjs`, `extract-webflow-page.mjs`, `localise-webflow-assets.mjs`,
> `check-assets.mjs`, `check-sitemap.mjs`, `sanity/test-converter.mjs`,
> `sanity/test-portable.mjs`, plus the added `check-cms-images.mjs`. Everything else in
> `migration-scripts/` is still byte-identical to the skill's copy.
